 during  peak conditions  even though they're still uh i think the  number was  16 000 megawatts of them that are forced  out  due to icing  okay the the next question was could you  provide an update on the numbers how  much  uh total generation is forced out and  what's the make up between  thermal and wind solar  yeah so the the the the total out at the  as of when we issued the news release  this morning was 40 000 megawatts of  total generation  including the installed capacity of the  wind about 23  500 of that is thermal generation  and the rest is is wind and solar  okay next question is from west  rappaport at nexstar  media group how close was the texas grid  to these catastrophic  blackouts that you have been talking  about we talking seconds minutes hours  days  certainly not days and i don't think  hours uh  you know when we our frequency went to a  level that  if our operators had not acted very  rapidly  uh to change the situation and arrest it  uh  it could it could have very quickly  changed and the reason i  i can't be as specific as seconds or  minutes  is that what was driving this uh  early monday morning sunday night was uh  seeing starting around 11 o'clock or  midnight uh  generation units coming off and then  more coming off in rapid succession as  the storm blew through  so one of the reasons that operators  have to act  uh to arrest the frequency is if they  say well you know let's wait another  minute and see what happens what happens  that next  minute might be three big units come off  and  then you're sunk uh so we were at a  level of frequency which  uh was uh one that needed to be  addressed immediately and that's what  the operators did  so i think you know if we hadn't taken  action it wouldn't have been that we  would have waited a few days and saw  what happened it was  uh seconds and minutes given the amount  of generation that was coming off the  system at the same time that the demand  was  still going uh up significantly so there  is a  system in place where uh uh it's called  under frequency load check that that  where there are what's called relays  there's devices  out across the transmission system that  if the if this power supply imbalance  becomes so severe that the frequency  drops  really fast really far and there's  different levels  that it will these will automatically  shed five percent  and then if it drops further they'll  check 10 and  and if they shed further it'll drop  it'll uh shed an additional 10 percent  so it's about that it's kind of a safety  net that if our  operators don't our operators and the  transmission owner operators aren't able  to shed  load in a controlled way which is what  we did  by choosing which circuits are are taken  out and  rotating through them and those kind of  things the safety net  should protect us for at least up to 25  percent loss of generation  but that's much more of a blunt  instrument it's harder to get the load  back faster  and it's not as controlled as what what  occurred one way or the other  is law of physics  the load is going to match the the  generation  and it's better to do that in a  controlled way like we did  and just let the safety net take it  the next question is from mark chediak  at bloomberg news  how has ercop been able to bring power  back so fast  did wind pick up is this largely from  the reactor coming back  or did it finally get warm enough for  the gas plants to work  so it's a it's a it's a variety of  things uh we've gotten  more generation back online of of  different types  we had more uh like during the afternoon  yesterday we had quite a bit of solar  generation online  so while that solar regeneration was  online we started trying to bring  back a lot of uh the load um  once we started in a very  deliberate manner starting to bring back  uh  and tell the transmission owners please  restore  a thousand megawatts of blood uh they  would go out and do the switching that  it would take to do that  and turn on different uh neighborhoods  that matched up to their share of that  thousand megawatts  and then once they were done we issue a  if we still had enough generation at on  at the time we'd issue a  order to go restore another thousand  megawatts and so we started to pick that  up  after we've done a few of those we  recognized that  we had more generation we had more  generation coming online and  the load that we were restoring per  feeder was less than what we expected  because businesses were shut down and  those kind of things and we realized at  that point that we could start to go  much faster  and so we that's that's really what  happened we started  telling the transmission operators to  restore this is kind of pretty much as  fast as you can because  we were we could keep up with it in  terms of generation  and that's why we were able i think this  was a question earlier that  by midnight last night we had uh told  the transmission owners  basically restore what what you can the  rest of what you can so we've worked  through that in steps  we started going a lot faster in the  late afternoon  and then uh kept going and then by  midnight we had kind of released the  uh to the to the level that they're  they're at at this point  so at this point how would urcot grade  itself during this  crisis please explain what grade and why  texans would like to know  this is from maya shea at abc 13.  you know we're still in the event we're  still  moving through you know the crisis that  was caused by this  uh i i just have to tell you it's a  little early um i will give  a good grade to the operators who took  those actions we just discussed to  prevent  a catastrophic blackout much worse  damage to our system  uh that was the i'd say the most  difficult decision that had to be made  throughout this whole event  uh was to do that rapidly and uh at the  size they did  because of the you know the loss of the  generation that we were seeing and the  increase in the load  so that that fundamental decision i  think was certainly sound  and uh had to be made i think you know  going forward  there's going to be a lot of  investigation assessment you know  examining  you know every step in this process and  and how our response  is uh i really think there will other  people ought to greatest uh you know and  we'll provide the information that's  needed to make those assessments and  uh want to learn from it i mean in the  business we're in we need to constantly  be  learning uh from uh events that we've  had  how to better utilize technology how to  better utilize  other tools to make the job of the  operators  even better so they can have more  options and take advantage of them so  i just say let us get through the event  get customers back on uh and then we can  take a look at  the details of our performance  okay next question uh moving forward  when  or will urcot winterize the system was  this the ultimate  issue this is from wfaa in dallas  i guess i'd say i have to step back and  say that  you know ercot is the uh the traffic cop  on the grid where the the air traffic  controller  managing the the flow of the electricity  we don't own the generation units we  don't own the transmission  uh we're really just managing the  overall uh  transmission system uh and uh  dispatching you know  putting generators on and off the grid  so  winterization is an effort that's going  to be need to be undertaken by those  entities that own the physical assets  out in the field  uh so we i the reason i said is we say  you know when will we winterize  it's really the the owners of the assets  that have to look at winterization best  practices and what are they going to do  to prevent issues like they saw  over the last few days i mean we're  willing to you know  work and help and make sure that those  are  effective and you know happy to work  with any of the industry on that but  it's really not our role to do  winterization  okay the next question is from leticia  miranda with nbc news  can you please address the issue of  electricity surcharges some consumers  have been charged high amounts for  electricity  t-puck said this is a result of a quirk  in urcot's system that has been  corrected can you please explain that  and how will it be corrected  right i've never heard anything about  that  yeah as customer search charges i have  i haven't heard of customer surcharges  related to this  um and we can't really speak to  the the surcharges the en-use customers  get  because and we're working in the  wholesale market and that's  separate in the retail market and we  just we don't have visibility into that  the public utility commission did issue  an order a couple of days ago  they had an emergency meeting to adjust  the wholesale scarcity pricing mechanism  to be sure that it was working  effectively to deliver as much  generation onto the system as possible  during this event  and we were able to to make just a  fix in our software program um  and how it treated uh those scarcity  prices to  implement uh the change that the public  utility commission thought was  appropriate  so as soon as they said they wanted to  make that policy change uh  you know we got right to it and  implemented in our system so it's  reflected in the wholesale  uh price that i know that the public  utility commission did issue an order  about this week  but again the retail surcharges i'm not  sure what the about that issue i'm just  not familiar with it  okay the next question is travis bubenik  with courthouse news  texas lawmaker dan crenshaw made a claim  widely shared on social media that  every natural gas plant stayed online  the downed plants were due to scheduled  maintenance  is that accurate  well i think like we've talked about  earlier there there were a few plants  that were out on scheduled maintenance  but  uh the plants the majority of the plants  that are that this number  that we've been reporting are plants  that went out either  fairly soon before the event because of  natural gas restrictions  uh or some of the icing of the turbines  or that tripped off lime  in the morning and then after the days  afterwards during the event  and the those generators in the  aggregate were  of various fuel types there was wind  generation  there was nuclear generation there was  coal generation there was gas fire  generation  all we have had uh units of each of  those types and and multiple units of  each of those types  i guess except for nuclear uh that  tripped offline  during the event  okay we have a little less than 10  minutes left  so i will try to get through as many  more questions  as i can the next one is from robert  arnold how fragile is the grid right now  are you concerned about plants going  down when more people come back on  yeah so i don't think we're we're not  worried about more plants going down  but cause more consumption uh if we if  just like we did before if we wind up  that if  more consumers you know like tonight  there's a lot more load as people start  turning things back  on as businesses reopen those kind of  things if that exceeds the amount of  available generation  we've said in the news release that we  could it's possible that we if that kind  of situation occurred we could have to  re-implement  rotating outages on a limited basis and  hopefully be able to rotate through  those as opposed to  having the longer term outages that have  happened before  um the uh the the  generation i think we we didn't lose  anywhere near as many plants tripping  offline  yesterday as what had happened for the  previous few days  so so it seems to be the as far as the  stability or the  kind of stability in the availability of  generation  seems to be much better now but one of  the things we're really watching is  making sure that those generators get  what they need  make sure that we're restoring power  back if there if  if there's because icing or something  like that there's there's not power to  natural gas compression stations and  those kind of things make sure we've got  plenty of pressure on the gas system  to continue to uh provide gas to the  electric generation make sure that we  get uh chemicals back to  the plants to make sure that they are uh  you know can uh use the chemicals they  need to keep that electric generation  online  and so that's really our focus right now  uh  but but to make sure that we don't have  additional problems occur that would  take generation offline  but for for what what this happened you  know yesterday afternoon and today we've  seen much less plants kind of tripping  for  weather release reasons or gas reasons  or anything like that so it seems  we've definitely seen an improvement  this is a follow up from platts would  you say that the improvement in  temperatures had as much  or more to do with the elimination of  widespread rolling outages than any  increase  in generation capacity  it probably had a bigger impact  than the availability of generation in  terms of  megawatts because like i said it's but  i'm not sure it was  weather or it was the fact that that as  i mentioned earlier as we had  individual as we started restoring load  there was less megawatts on each feeder  that had occurred  uh when when the load those feeders were  originally turned off  and that that less load was a function  of you know  things being shut down less less  consumption and then also  there's some extent it was due to the  more of  less cold temperature so as a  combination  okay this is a follow-up from nbc news  uh  why did the board of directors list  disappear  from the urcot website looks like the  link to it is gone  and does ercot have a response to the  governor saying on  a local abc station that leadership  should resign  on the website issue  the publicly available information for  those board of directors members  was being used to send uh sometimes  harassing  uh communications  and threats were being made to the board  members  we were very concerned about both  employee and  board member safety basically because of  the things we've been receiving  over the past few days so  the thought was let's pull that contact  information down but i think on  reflection  as we after we did that i mean this was  something going on  you know sort of in the background  because it's certainly not related to  getting power back on the grid uh but  on reflection we figured that  information ought to be up there and i  think  i think we leslie do you know if we've  replaced it  back or not uh i i know that the  information will be back up before next  week so  i'll check that yeah yeah so it was a  it was a security safety idea  uh but i think as as people observed um  you know that's that's it was public  information uh  uh that uh has been on the website in  the past so we think it's it makes sense  to  go ahead and get that back up there  okay i think we've got time for sorry i  didn't  leave out your second part i think that  you know the the idea of changing the  governance changing the board of  directors  the the uh uh oversight of ercot  actually sort of the creation and  purpose and  uh what we're supposed to be doing all  of that is  reflected in texas statutes in texas law  and as uh the energy markets have  evolved as the expectations of ercot  have evolved and changed  that's addressed by the legislature and  then by the public utility commission  i think if there's dissatisfaction with  how  ercot's board governance process works  uh  it sounds like that will be part of the  review that comes after  uh we get the number one priority of  getting people's lights back on after  that's done  and we you know welcome that discussion  but it's a discussion that you know  however the  legislature resolves it that's what  we'll do um that's  that's how we operate because the  composition of our board is  is defined in statute and if the  legislature thinks that needs to change  then that's certainly something we'll  we'll follow  okay and a final question from the star  telegram  there's been policy debate about  renewable versus thermal energy sources  from texas  leaders in light of this weather event  what do you all think the texas  legislature should look at when it comes  to energy policy and sources  well again um the policy debate  uh is not one that we're focused on  right now  uh i'm sure you know this event uh  which called so much attention to the to  the energy to the electric system  uh is going to be discussed debated and  analyzed uh  starting next week the legislature is  going to start having hearings next week  about that  so i think there's you know there was a  robust debate about energy policy in a  lot of quarters  uh you know well before these events and  i think these events have really put a  sharp focus on them so i think you'll  see legislative debate about that  and you know again kind of back to  urcot's role  the state asks us to implement  state law and to implement measures to  make sure that the grid is reliable and  doesn't have a blackout  and if there are changes in what the  legislature wants ercot to do  as part of that that again you know we  need to follow the state statute because  that's  uh that's you know our governing  document when it comes down to it  okay well i think we are just about at  the top of the hour  so i am going to conclude this media  call but we will  continue to bring you updates uh later  in the day  and uh we appreciate you joining us and  thank you bill and thank you dan  i know you two are busy and tired and uh  we do appreciate you communicating with  the media  so thank you so much thank you all happy  to be here
 

